\description{
Time series data is often influenced by serial correlation. When data are not random and influenced by autocorrelation, modified Mann-Kendall tests may be used for trend detction. Yue and Wang (2004) have proposed variance correction approach to address the issue of serial correlation in trend analysis. Data are initially detrended and the effective sample size is calculated from the sample serial correlation coefficients of the detrended series at lags 1 to \code{lag.max}. No significance screening is applied to these coefficients.
}
\details{
The variance correction approach suggested by Yue and Wang (2004) is implemeted in this function. By default (\code{lag.max = NULL}), sample serial correlation coefficients for all lags 1 to n-1 are used in calculating the effective sample size.

Caution: with all lags and no significance screening, the correction factor n/n* is strongly biased low (for a series with no trend and no autocorrelation its expected value is about 1/3 before detrending, and smaller after Sen detrending), so the corrected test rejects the null hypothesis far too often. In simulations of trend-free white noise, about 45-50 percent of series are declared significant at the 5 percent level (O'Brien et al., 2021; Militino et al., 2020; Morrissey, 2026). With \code{lag.max = 1}, or with \code{mmky1lag}, the white-noise rejection rate is close to nominal (about 6-9 percent for n = 30-100); each additional lag inflates it further. If the estimated n/n* is not positive, the corrected variance is undefined and NaN is returned.
}
